-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 16 -- security for costs`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:2364 | 16. —(1) The appellant must provide security for the respondent’s costs of the appeal and file a certificate for security for costs in Form 36 at the time the appellant files the notice of appeal. (2) Where there is more than one appellant in the same appeal, all the appellants need to provide only one set of security for the appeal.
-- src:2365 | (3) Where there is more than one respondent, the appellant must provide security for the costs of the appeal for each respondent (or for the costs of the appeal of each set of the respondents where the respondents are represented by the same firm of solicitors).
-- src:2366 | (4) The security must be — ( a )
-- src:2367 | in the form of a solicitor’s undertaking in Form 37 which must be filed and served on the respondent;
-- src:2368 | ( b )
-- src:2369 | deposited in the Registry or with the Accountant‑General; or
-- src:2370 | ( c )
-- src:2371 | in any other form acceptable to the parties.
-- src:2372 | (5) The appellant must provide security in the following amounts: ( a )
-- src:2373 | $3,000 for an appeal against a judgment in a Magistrate’s Court action;
-- src:2374 | ( b )
-- src:2375 | $5,000 for an appeal against a judgment in a District Court action.
-- src:2376 | (6) Any party may apply to the appellate Court to vary or waive the amount of security for costs to be provided.
-- src:2377 | (7) The appellate Court may order further security for costs to be given.
@ref O 19 r 16 (src:2364-2377)
GIVEN `the appellant has provided security for the respondent's costs of the appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the certificate for security for costs in Form 36 is filed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `both are done at the time the appellant files the notice of appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(1) the appellant has provided security for costs and filed the certificate as required` MEANS
    IF `the appellant has provided security for the respondent's costs of the appeal` AND `the certificate for security for costs in Form 36 is filed` AND `both are done at the time the appellant files the notice of appeal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The appellant must provide security for the respondent’s costs of the appeal and file a certificate for security for costs in Form 36 at the time the appellant files the notice of appeal"

@ref O 19 r 16(2) (src:2364)
GIVEN `there is more than one appellant in the same appeal` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(2) the appellants need to provide only one set of security for the appeal` MEANS
    IF `there is more than one appellant in the same appeal`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where there is more than one appellant in the same appeal" -- a "where" that is not met

@ref O 19 r 16(3) (src:2365)
GIVEN `there is more than one respondent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(3) the appellant must provide security for each respondent or each set of respondents` MEANS
    IF `there is more than one respondent`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where there is more than one respondent" -- a "where" that is not met

-- The number of sets: one for each respondent, or for each set of respondents represented by the same firm of solicitors.
-- Both facts are asked: "more than one respondent" is met by two respondents of one firm, who make one set.
@ref O 19 r 16(3) (src:2365)
GIVEN `the number of respondents` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of sets of respondents, where respondents represented by the same firm of solicitors are one set` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`o 19 r 16(3) the number of sets of security the appellant must provide` MEANS
    IF `the number of respondents` GREATER THAN 1
    THEN JUST `the number of sets of respondents, where respondents represented by the same firm of solicitors are one set`
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): paragraph (3) speaks of "more than one respondent"; with one, paragraph (1) gives security for the respondent's costs

DECLARE `o 19 r 16(4) form of security` IS ONE OF
    `o 19 r 16(4)(a) a solicitor's undertaking in Form 37`
    `o 19 r 16(4)(b) a deposit in the Registry or with the Accountant-General`
    `o 19 r 16(4)(c) any other form`

@ref O 19 r 16(4) (src:2366-2371)
GIVEN form IS A `o 19 r 16(4) form of security`
      `the undertaking is filed and served on the respondent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the form is acceptable to the parties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(4) the security is in a form the text allows` MEANS
    CONSIDER form
    WHEN `o 19 r 16(4)(a) a solicitor's undertaking in Form 37` THEN
        IF `the undertaking is filed and served on the respondent`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in the form of a solicitor’s undertaking in Form 37 which must be filed and served on the respondent"
    WHEN `o 19 r 16(4)(b) a deposit in the Registry or with the Accountant-General` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `o 19 r 16(4)(c) any other form` THEN
        IF `the form is acceptable to the parties`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The security must be — ... (c) in any other form acceptable to the parties"

-- Paragraph (5): $3,000 for an appeal against a judgment in a Magistrate's Court action, $5,000 for a District Court action.
`o 19 r 16(5)(a) the amount of security for a Magistrate's Court action` MEANS 3000
`o 19 r 16(5)(b) the amount of security for a District Court action` MEANS 5000

@ref O 19 r 16(5) (src:2372-2375)
GIVEN court IS A `Court level`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`o 19 r 16(5) the amount of security the appellant must provide` MEANS
    CONSIDER court
    WHEN `Magistrate's Court` THEN JUST `o 19 r 16(5)(a) the amount of security for a Magistrate's Court action`
    WHEN `District Court` THEN JUST `o 19 r 16(5)(b) the amount of security for a District Court action`
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (5) gives amounts for a Magistrate's Court action and a District Court action only as to those two courts

@ref O 19 r 16(5) (src:2372-2375)
GIVEN court IS A `Court level`
      `the amount of security provided` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(5) the security is in the amount the text requires` MEANS
    CONSIDER `o 19 r 16(5) the amount of security the appellant must provide` court
    WHEN JUST required THEN
        IF `the amount of security provided` EQUALS required
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The appellant must provide security in the following amounts: (a) $3,000 for an appeal against a judgment in a Magistrate’s Court action; (b) $5,000 for an appeal against a judgment in a District Court action"
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (5) names only the Magistrate's Court and the District Court

@ref O 19 r 16(6) (src:2376)
GIVEN `the person is a party` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(6) a party may apply to the appellate Court to vary or waive the amount of security` MEANS
    IF `the person is a party`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (6) speaks only of "Any party"

@ref O 19 r 16(7) (src:2377)
GIVEN `the order is for further security for costs to be given` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 16(7) the appellate Court may order further security for costs to be given` MEANS
    IF `the order is for further security for costs to be given`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph (7) gives the power to order "further security for costs"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 17 -- documents to be filed`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

`o 19 r 17(3) the period for written grounds of decision` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 12
    `period unit` IS Weeks
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 17(3)(b) the period of notice to strike out` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 17(4) the period for the appellant's documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 28
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 17(7)(a) the period for the request for directions` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 17(8) the period for the respondent's documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 28
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 19 r 17(10) the period for the appellant's reply documents` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

-- src:2379 | 17. —(1) The lower Court must issue its written grounds of decision after the notice of appeal has been filed if the lower Court has not already done so. (2) The Registry must notify the parties when the record of proceedings is ready for collection.
-- src:2380 | (3) If no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks after the date of filing of the notice of appeal — ( a )
-- src:2381 | the appellant must apply in writing to the Registrar to proceed with the appeal and for a copy of the record of proceedings;
-- src:2382 | ( b )
-- src:2383 | if the appellant fails to do so, the respondent may apply in writing to the Registrar to proceed with the appeal and for a copy of the record of proceedings, or may give the appellant 14 days’ written notice of the respondent’s intention to strike out the appeal; and
-- src:2384 | ( c )
-- src:2385 | after the expiry of the 14 days’ notice period mentioned in sub‑paragraph ( b ), the respondent may apply to strike out the appeal. [S 37/2024 wef 01/02/2024]
-- src:2386 | (4) The appellant must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2387 | the record of appeal;
-- src:2388 | ( b )
-- src:2389 | the appellant’s Case;
-- src:2390 | ( c )
-- src:2391 | the appellant’s core bundle of documents, with the written judgment or grounds of decision of the lower Court and the extracted order of the lower Court in a separate volume; and
-- src:2392 | ( d )
-- src:2393 | the appellant’s bundle of authorities,
-- src:2394 | within 28 days after the date on which the Registry informs the parties that the record of proceedings is available.
-- src:2395 | (5) Where the appellant fails to file and serve the record of appeal, the core bundle of documents or the appellant’s Case within the specified time, the appeal is deemed withdrawn unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2396 | (6) Where an appeal is deemed withdrawn pursuant to paragraph (5) and if all the parties to the appeal consent to the payment of the security for costs provided under Rule 16 to the appellant or the respondent, the appellant or the respondent (as the case may be) must file the document signifying such consent signed by the parties and in such event, the security for costs provided under Rule 16 must be paid to the appellant or the respondent (as the case may be) and any solicitor’s undertaking is discharged.
-- src:2397 | (7) Where an appeal is deemed withdrawn pursuant to paragraph (5) and if there are any outstanding issues as to costs or other matters that remain between the parties to the appeal — ( a )
-- src:2398 | the appellant or any party to the appeal may, within 14 days after the date that the appeal is deemed withdrawn, request in writing to the General Division for directions on those issues;
-- src:2399 | ( b )
-- src:2400 | no oral arguments are to be made in a request under sub‑paragraph ( a ) unless the appellate Court otherwise directs; and
-- src:2401 | ( c )
-- src:2402 | the Registrar of the Supreme Court may, upon receiving a request under sub‑paragraph ( a ), give directions on the making of written submissions for the request.
-- src:2403 | (8) The respondent must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2404 | the respondent’s Case;
-- src:2405 | ( b )
-- src:2406 | the respondent’s core bundle of documents (if necessary); and
-- src:2407 | ( c )
-- src:2408 | the respondent’s bundle of authorities,
-- src:2409 | within 28 days after the appellant serves the documents mentioned in paragraph (4) on the respondent.
-- src:2410 | (9) Where the respondent fails to file and serve the respondent’s Case within the specified time, the respondent is not allowed to make submissions at the hearing of the appeal unless the appellate Court otherwise orders.
-- src:2411 | (10) The appellant must file and serve — ( a )
-- src:2412 | the appellant’s Reply (if any);
-- src:2413 | ( b )
-- src:2414 | the second core bundle (if necessary); and
-- src:2415 | ( c )
-- src:2416 | the appellant’s second bundle of authorities (if any),
-- src:2417 | within 14 days after the respondent’s Case is served on the appellant.
-- src:2418 | (11) Where there is more than one appellant in an appeal, all the appellants may join in one appellants’ Case and in one appellants’ Reply.
-- src:2419 | (12) Where there is more than one respondent in an appeal, all the respondents may join in one respondents’ Case.
-- src:2420 | (13) A party whose interest in the appeal is passive (such as a stakeholder, a trustee or an executor) is not required to file a separate Case but should ensure that that party’s position is explained in one of the Cases filed.
-- src:2421 | (14) The appellant and the respondent may seek directions from the General Division to file a joint Case where there are special circumstances.
-- src:2422 | (15) All parties to 2 or more appeals to be heard together must try to agree on filing a single Case for each party and on the timelines for such filing.
-- src:2423 | (16) Where the parties are unable to agree as mentioned in paragraph (15), they must request in writing for a case conference before the General Division or seek directions from the General Division.
-- src:2424 | (17) Where the parties have agreed as mentioned in paragraph (15), they must inform the Registrar in writing of the timelines agreed, and seek the approval of the timelines by the General Division.
-- src:2425 | (18) Where there are 2 or more appeals arising from the same judgment, the parties must file a joint record of appeal.
-- src:2426 | (19) No documents other than what have been set out in this Rule may be filed unless the General Division otherwise orders.
-- src:2427 | (20) No written submissions or skeletal arguments may be filed before or at the appeal unless the General Division otherwise orders.
@ref O 19 r 17 (src:2379-2427)
GIVEN `the notice of appeal has been filed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the lower Court has already issued its written grounds of decision` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 17(1) the lower Court must issue its written grounds of decision` MEANS
    IF `the notice of appeal has been filed` AND NOT (`the lower Court has already issued its written grounds of decision`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "after the notice of appeal has been filed if the lower Court has not already done so" -- an "after" and an "if" that are not met

@ref O 19 r 17(2) (src:2379)
GIVEN `the record of proceedings is ready for collection` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 17(2) the Registry must notify the parties that the record of proceedings is ready for collection` MEANS
    IF `the record of proceedings is ready for collection`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "The Registry must notify the parties when the record of proceedings is ready for collection" -- a "when" that is not met

-- Paragraph (3): no written grounds within 12 weeks after the date of filing of the notice of appeal.
-- The last day of the 12 weeks is O 3 r 3's answer (input).
@ref O 19 r 17(3) (src:2380-2385)
GIVEN `the date the written grounds of decision are issued` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the date asked about` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 19 r 17(3) no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the date the written grounds of decision are issued`
    WHEN JUST issued THEN
        IF issued AT MOST `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If no written grounds of decision are issued within 12 weeks after the date of filing of the notice of appeal" -- they were issued within the 12 weeks
        ELSE JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        IF `the date asked about` GREATER THAN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the 12 weeks have not run out and no grounds are issued yet

